RESEARCHER_FLINT_TRACE_JSON {"content": "1: # Subject Flint laboratory API\n2: \n3: Your Python file runs with these prebound names: `lab`, `json`, `re`, and `math`.\n4: Imports and direct filesystem access are intentionally unavailable.\n5: \n6: ## `lab.scan(layers, corpus_passages=384, candidates_per_layer_pole=8, tail_contexts=6)`\n7: Collect natural routed-expert neuron activations from Wikipedia and write full candidate contexts to\n8: `activation_candidates_scan_N.jsonl` plus compact `activation_cards_scan_N.txt`. Choose 1–4 distinct\n9: layers from `[8, 12, 20, 28, 32, 36, 40]`. At most two scans; 192–512 passages per scan.\n10: \n11: ## `lab.baseline(prompts, max_new_tokens=48)`\n12: Generate unmodified subject outputs for 1–16 distinct prompts.\n13: \n14: ## `lab.intervene(candidate_id, factor, prompts, reverse=False, max_new_tokens=48)`\n15: While predicting the first generated token, add the chosen signed neuron's unit-normalized output\n16: direction at `factor` strength. `reverse=True` applies the opposite pole. This bypasses natural expert\n17: routing and is a causal write-direction intervention, not proof of the neuron's ordinary function.\n18: \n19: ## `lab.matched_control(candidate_id)`\n20: Return the same-layer scanned candidate with closest output-vector norm.\n21: \n22: ## `lab.confirm(candidate_id, factor, prompts, regex, scope=\"whole\", max_new_tokens=48)`\n23: On at least eight prompts, run target, baseline, opposite-pole, and matched-neuron conditions. You\n24: choose the candidate, factor, fresh prompts, regex, scope, and interpretation. Returns raw outputs and\n25: match counts; it imposes no success threshold. Confirmation prompts must not themselves contain text\n26: matched by the scoring regex. The matched control is also returned.\n27: \n28: Every operation and raw output is appended immutably to `lab_calls.jsonl`. Use ordinary Python\n29: analysis in your script, and use workspace read/grep tools to inspect the generated evidence.", "kind": "tool_result", "name": "read_lines", "step": 1}
